Acute hemolytic transfusion reaction - wikipedia Acute hemolytic transfusion reaction Jump to : navigation , search This article may be too technical for most readers to understand . Please help improve it to make it understandable to non-experts , without removing the technical details . ( January 2018 ) ( Learn how and when to remove this template message ) An acute hemolytic transfusion reaction ( AHTR ) is a type of transfusion reaction that is associated with hemolysis . It occurs very soon after the transfusion , and within 24 hrs post-transfusion . It can occur quickly upon transfusing a few milliliters , or up to 1 -- 2 hours post-transfusion . It is also known as an `` immediate hemolytic transfusion reaction '' . This is a medical emergency as it results from rapid destruction of the donor red blood cells by host antibodies ( IgG , IgM ) . It is usually related to ABO blood group incompatibility - the most severe of which often involves group A red cells being given to a patient with group O type blood .","entities":["transfusion reaction","hemolysis","medical emergency","donor red blood cells","host antibodies","IgG","IgM","ABO blood group incompatibility","group A red cells","group O type blood","patient","transfusion","Acute hemolytic transfusion reaction"],"facts":["Acute hemolytic transfusion reaction -RELATED_TO-> alternate pathway cascade","Acute hemolytic transfusion reaction -RELATED_TO-> intravascular haemolysis","donor cells -RELATED_TO-> IgG","non-immune mediated reactions -RELATED_TO-> transfusion","Acute hemolytic transfusion reaction -RELATED_TO-> extravascular haemolysis"]}
