By contrast , seedless watermelons are grown from seeds . These seeds are produced by crossing diploid and tetraploid lines of watermelon , with the resulting seeds producing sterile triploid plants . Fruit development is triggered by pollination , so these plants must be grown alongside a diploid strain to provide pollen . Triploid plants with seedless fruits can also be produced using endosperm culture for the regeneration of triploid plantlets from endosperm tissue via somatic embryogenesis . One disadvantage of most seedless crops is a significant reduction in the amount of genetic diversity in the species . As genetically identical clones , a pest or disease that affects one individual is likely capable of affecting each of its clones . For example , the vast majority of commercially produced bananas are cloned from a single source , the Cavendish cultivar , and those plants are currently threatened worldwide by a newly discovered fungal disease to which they are highly susceptible . References ( edit ) Jump up ^ Frost , H.B. , Soost , R.K. ( 1968 ) `` Seed reproduction : development of gametes and embryos '' . In : Reuther , W. Webber , H.J.","entities":["Seedless fruit","seedless watermelons","seeds","diploid lines","tetraploid lines","sterile triploid plants","fruit development","diploid strain","endosperm culture","triploid plantlets","endosperm tissue","somatic embryogenesis","seedless crops","clones","pest"],"facts":["Seedless fruit -RELATED_TO-> watermelons","Seedless fruit -RELATED_TO-> tomatoes","Seedless fruit -RELATED_TO-> bananas","Seedless fruit -RELATED_TO-> seedless citrus fruits","Seedless fruit -RELATED_TO-> Seedless watermelon"]}
