Lithium hydroxide is a strong base and , when heated with a fat , produces a soap made of lithium stearate . Lithium soap has the ability to thicken oils , and it is used to manufacture all - purpose , high - temperature lubricating greases . Metallurgy ( edit ) Lithium ( e.g. as lithium carbonate ) is used as an additive to continuous casting mould flux slags where it increases fluidity , a use which accounts for 5 % of global lithium use ( 2011 ) . Lithium compounds are also used as additives ( fluxes ) to foundry sand for iron casting to reduce veining . Lithium ( as lithium fluoride ) is used as an additive to aluminium smelters ( Hall -- Héroult process ) , reducing melting temperature and increasing electrical resistance , a use which accounts for 3 % of production ( 2011 ) . When used as a flux for welding or soldering , metallic lithium promotes the fusing of metals during the process and eliminates the forming of oxides by absorbing impurities .","entities":["metals","Lithium hydroxide","Lithium soap","Lithium stearate","All-purpose, high-temperature lubricating greases","Continuous casting mould flux slags","Fluidity","Foundry sand","Iron casting","Veining","Aluminium smelters","Hall–Héroult process","Electrical resistance","Welding","Impurities"],"facts":["Lithium carbonate -RELATED_TO-> ovenware","Lithium -PART_OF-> metals","metals -PART_OF-> By metallicity","Lithium carbonate -RELATED_TO-> lithium oxides","Lithium carbonate -RELATED_TO-> Oxygen candles"]}
