Of the 711 passengers and crew rescued by the Carpathia , six , including first class passenger William F. Hoyt , either died in a lifeboat during the night or on board the Carpathia the next morning , and were buried at sea . In the days following the sinking , several ships sailed to the disaster area to recover victims ' bodies . The White Star Line chartered the cable ship Mackay - Bennett from Halifax , Nova Scotia to retrieve bodies . Three other ships followed in the search : the cable ship Minia , the lighthouse supply ship Montmagny and the sealing vessel Algerine . Each ship left with embalming supplies , undertakers , and clergy . Upon recovery , each body retrieved by the Mackay - Bennett was numbered and given as detailed a description as possible to help aid in identification . The physical appearance of each body -- height , weight , age , hair and eye colour , visible birthmarks , scars or tattoos , was catalogued and any personal effects on the bodies were gathered and placed in small canvas bags corresponding to their number .","entities":["White Star Line","Carpathia","William F. Hoyt","Mackay-Bennett","Halifax","disaster area","Minia","Montmagny","Algerine","search","711 passengers and crew","sinking","RMS Titanic","Nova Scotia"],"facts":["White Star Line -RELATED_TO-> steerage passengers","White Star Line -RELATED_TO-> stroller","an agency -RELATED_TO-> White Star Line","Cunard -RELATED_TO-> White Star Line","J. Bruce Ismay -WORKS_FOR-> White Star Line"]}
