Foreign relations of Australia - Wikipedia Foreign relations of Australia Jump to : navigation , search Foreign relations of Australia are influenced by its position as a leading trading nation and as a significant donor of humanitarian aid . Australia 's foreign policy is guided by a commitment to multilateralism and regionalism , as well as to strong bilateral relations with its allies . Key concerns include free trade , terrorism , refugees , economic co-operation with Asia and stability in the Asia - Pacific . Australia is active in the United Nations and the Commonwealth of Nations . Given its history of starting and supporting important regional and global initiatives , it has been described as a regional middle power par excellence . Australia 's imports of major weapons increased 65 per cent between 2005 -- 2009 and 2010 -- 14 , making it the sixth largest importer in the world according to SIPRI . It has become steadfastly allied with New Zealand , through long - standing ties dating back to the 1800s , as well as the United States , throughout the Cold War and since . Over recent decades Australia has sought to strengthen its relationship with Asian countries , with this becoming the focus of the country 's network of diplomatic missions .","entities":["New Zealand","United States","Stability","Cold War","Asia-Pacific","SIPRI","major weapons","multilateralism","regionalism","economic co-operation","humanitarian aid","regional middle power","Commonwealth of Nations","Australia","Refugees"],"facts":["New Zealand -LOCATED_IN-> Oceania","two-way contacts between California and New Zealand -LOCATED_IN-> New Zealand","90-minute two-way contact between New Zealand and England -LOCATED_IN-> New Zealand","Discovery of islands -LOCATED_IN-> New Zealand","New Zealand -LOCATED_IN-> Southwest Pacific"]}
[E80d0b5] {"title":"Foreign relations of Australia","text":"Foreign relations of Australia
Australia was a participant at the inaugural ASEAN sponsored East Asia Summit in 2005 . Australia 's place at the summit was only secured after it agreed to reverse its policy and accede to ASEAN 's Treaty of Amity and Cooperation in Southeast Asia . Australia had been reluctant to sign the treaty out of concerns regarding how it would affect Australia 's obligation under other treaty arrangements including ANZUS . Papua New Guinea ( PNG ) , a former Australian territory , is the largest recipient of Australian assistance . Starting in 1997 -- 99 Australia contributed to the IMF program for Thailand and assisted Indonesia and PNG with regional environmental crisis and drought relief efforts . Security treaties ( edit ) Australia is party to the Australia , New Zealand , United States security treaty . It has also been a party of the Australia , New Zealand , United Kingdom force . Trade ( edit ) Overall Australia 's largest trading partners are the United States , Korea , Japan , China , and the United Kingdom . Australia currently has bilateral Free Trade Agreements with New Zealand , the United States , Thailand and Singapore as of 2007 .","entities":["New Zealand","United States","Korea","East Asia Summit","Treaty of Amity and Cooperation in Southeast Asia","Other treaty arrangements","ANZUS","Papua New Guinea","IMF program for Thailand","Regional environmental crisis","Drought relief efforts","Australia, New Zealand, United States security treaty","Australia, New Zealand, United Kingdom force","Bilateral Free Trade Agreements","United Kingdom"],"facts":["New Zealand -LOCATED_IN-> Oceania","two-way contacts between California and New Zealand -LOCATED_IN-> New Zealand","90-minute two-way contact between New Zealand and England -LOCATED_IN-> New Zealand","Discovery of islands -LOCATED_IN-> New Zealand","New Zealand -LOCATED_IN-> Southwest Pacific"]}
