Bombing in the Second World War destroyed much of the stadium ; the central tunnel in the South Stand was all that remained of that quarter . After the war , the club received compensation from the War Damage Commission in the amount of £ 22,278 . While reconstruction took place , the team played its `` home '' games at Manchester City 's Maine Road ground ; Manchester United was charged £ 5,000 per year , plus a nominal percentage of gate receipts . Later improvements included the addition of roofs , first to the Stretford End and then to the North and East Stands . The roofs were supported by pillars that obstructed many fans ' views , and they were eventually replaced with a cantilevered structure . The Stretford End was the last stand to receive a cantilevered roof , completed in time for the 1993 -- 94 season . First used on 25 March 1957 and costing £ 40,000 , four 180 - foot ( 55 m ) pylons were erected , each housing 54 individual floodlights .","entities":["East Stand","War Damage Commission","Maine Road","Stretford End","1993–94 season","four 180-foot pylons","54 individual floodlights","cantilevered roof","North Stand","Manchester City","Manchester United F.C.","Second World War"],"facts":["North Stand -LOCATED_IN-> Old Trafford","East Stand -PART_OF-> Madejski Stadium","Planning application to extend the ground -RELATED_TO-> East Stand","East Stand -LOCATED_IN-> Old Trafford","North Stand renamed Eamonn Dolan Stand -RELATED_TO-> North Stand"]}
