Contents ( hide ) 1 History 2 Process 2.1 Sources of hydrogen 2.2 Reaction rate and equilibrium 2.3 Catalysts 3 Economic and environmental aspects 4 See also 5 References 6 External links History ( edit ) Main article : History of the Haber process Throughout the 19th century the demand for nitrates and ammonia for use as fertilizers and industrial feedstocks had been steadily increasing . The main source was mining niter deposits . At the beginning of the 20th century it was being predicted that these reserves could not satisfy future demand and research into new potential sources of ammonia became more important . The obvious source was atmospheric nitrogen ( N ) , comprising nearly 80 % of the air , however N is exceptionally stable and will not readily react with other chemicals . Converting N into ammonia posed a challenge for chemists globally . Haber , with his assistant Robert Le Rossignol , developed the high - pressure devices and catalysts needed to demonstrate the Haber process at laboratory scale .","entities":["air","Haber process","ammonia","Haber","Robert Le Rossignol","nitrates","fertilizers","industrial feedstocks","niter deposits","atmospheric nitrogen (N)","high-pressure devices","catalysts"],"facts":["air -RELATED_TO-> demonstration of the Haber process","Hops -RELATED_TO-> air","Scandium -RELATED_TO-> air","OpenFL -RELATED_TO-> air","Haber process -RELATED_TO-> demonstration of the Haber process"]}
