This exclusion is not affected by changes subsequent to the Perth Agreement . History ( edit ) This section needs additional citations for verification . Please help improve this article by adding citations to reliable sources . Unsourced material may be challenged and removed . ( May 2015 ) ( Learn how and when to remove this template message ) The current succession law in the United Kingdom evolved from succession law in both England and Scotland . Originally in both countries , there were no fixed rules governing succession to the throne . The individual could have relied on inheritance , statute , election ( by Parliament or by another body ) , nomination ( by a reigning sovereign in his or her will ) , conquest or prescription ( de facto possession of the Crown ) . It was often unclear which of these bases should take precedence ; often , the outcome depended not on the legal strength of the claims , but on the political or military power of the claimants . However , over time , the default rule became male primogeniture : later monarchs coming to the throne by exception to this rule went to great lengths to explain and justify going against these rules , and to prove their rivals illegitimate . Eventually , Parliament asserted control of succession .","entities":["statute","Scotland","England","United Kingdom","Perth Agreement","Succession law in the United Kingdom","Succession law in England","Succession law in Scotland","Election","Nomination","Male primogeniture","Succession to the British throne","inheritance","Parliament","prescription"],"facts":["maximum speed permitted by statute -RELATED_TO-> statute","Scotland -LOCATED_IN-> UK","California rules of professional conduct -RELATED_TO-> statute","violation of the statute -RELATED_TO-> statute","Scotland -LOCATED_IN-> Europe"]}

