[E362846] {"title":"Sino-Indian War","text":"Sino-Indian War
The CIA later revealed that at that time the Chinese had neither the fuel nor runways long enough for using their air force effectively in Tibet . Indians in general became highly sceptical of China and its military . Many Indians view the war as a betrayal of India 's attempts at establishing a long - standing peace with China and started to question the once popular `` Hindi - Chini bhai - bhai '' ( meaning `` Indians and Chinese are brothers '' ) . The war also put an end to Nehru 's earlier hopes that India and China would form a strong Asian Axis to counteract the increasing influence of the Cold War bloc superpowers . The unpreparedness of the army was blamed on Defence Minister Menon , who resigned his government post to allow for someone who might modernise India 's military further . India 's policy of weaponisation via indigenous sources and self - sufficiency was thus cemented . Sensing a weakened army , Pakistan , a close ally of China , began a policy of provocation against India by infiltrating Jammu and Kashmir and ultimately triggering the Second Kashmir War with India in 1965 and Indo - Pakistani war of 1971 .","entities":["Cold War","India","Pakistan","China","Tibet","Nehru","Asian Axis","Second Kashmir War","Indo-Pakistani War of 1971","Chinese military","Hindi-Chini bhai-bhai","peace with China","Cold War bloc superpowers","India's military","weaponisation"],"facts":["Another Such Victory: President Truman and the Cold War, 1945–2002 -RELATED_TO-> Cold War","Dean Acheson: A Life in the Cold War -RELATED_TO-> Cold War","Proclaiming the Truman Doctrine: The Cold War Call to Arms -RELATED_TO-> Cold War","Congress and the Origins of the Cold War: The Truman Doctrine -RELATED_TO-> Cold War","Journal of Cold War Studies -RELATED_TO-> Cold War"]}
