In other respects , the quantity and quality of experience are important ; for example , there is substantial evidence that animals raised in enriched environments have thicker cerebral cortices , indicating a higher density of synaptic connections , than animals whose levels of stimulation are restricted . Physiology. The functions of the brain depend on the ability of neurons to transmit electrochemical signals to other cells , and their ability to respond appropriately to electrochemical signals received from other cells . The electrical properties of neurons are controlled by a wide variety of biochemical and metabolic processes , most notably the interactions between neurotransmitters and receptors that take place at synapses . Neurotransmitters and receptors Neurotransmitters are chemicals that are released at synapses when an action potential activates them -- neurotransmitters attach themselves to receptor molecules on the membrane of the synapse 's target cell , and thereby alter the electrical or chemical properties of the receptor molecules . With few exceptions , each neuron in the brain releases the same chemical neurotransmitter , or combination of neurotransmitters , at all the synaptic connections it makes with other neurons ; this rule is known as Dale 's principle . Thus , a neuron can be characterized by the neurotransmitters that it releases .","entities":["Receptors","Synapses","Membrane","Experience","enriched environments","cerebral cortices","neurons","electrochemical signals","biochemical and metabolic processes","electrical properties of neurons","neurotransmitters","receptor molecules","target cell","Dale's principle","Brain"],"facts":["Synapses -RELATED_TO-> photoreceptor cells","olfactory bulb -RELATED_TO-> Receptors","Receptors -RELATED_TO-> GABA","Synapses -LOCATED_IN-> nervous system","Synapses -RELATED_TO-> ganglion cells"]}

