Most bones have only one primary center ( e.g. all long bones ) but some irregular bones such as the os coxa ( hip ) and vertebrae have multiple primary centers . A secondary ossification center is the area of ossification that appears after the primary ossification center has already appeared -- most of which appear during the postnatal and adolescent years . Most bones have more than one secondary ossification center . In long bones , the secondary centres appear in the epiphyses . References ( edit ) This article incorporates text in the public domain from the 20th edition of Gray 's Anatomy ( 1918 ) Jump up ^ Gray and Spitzka ( 1910 ) , page 44 . Bibliography ( edit ) Gray , Henry ; Spitzka , Edward Anthony ( 1910 ) . Anatomy , descriptive and applied . the University of California : Lea & Febiger .","entities":["primary ossification center","long bones","bones","secondary ossification center","os coxa","vertebrae","epiphyses","postnatal years","adolescent years","Ossification center","Henry Gray","Edward Anthony Spitzka","Anatomy, descriptive and applied","Lea & Febiger","Gray's Anatomy"],"facts":["primary ossification center -LOCATED_IN-> diaphysis","primary ossification center -LOCATED_IN-> central part of a developing bone","primary ossification center -RELATED_TO-> Prenatal development","primary ossification center -LOCATED_IN-> body of the bone","primary ossification center -PART_OF-> bone"]}
