Determining costs requires keeping records of goods or materials purchased and any discounts on such purchase . In addition , if the goods are modified , the business must determine the costs incurred in modifying the goods . Such modification costs include labor , supplies or additional material , supervision , quality control and use of equipment . Principles for determining costs may be easily stated , but application in practice is often difficult due to a variety of considerations in the allocation of costs . Cost of goods sold may also reflect adjustments . Among the potential adjustments are decline in value of the goods ( i.e. , lower market value than cost ) , obsolescence , damage , etc . When multiple goods are bought or made , it may be necessary to identify which costs relate to which particular goods sold . This may be done using an identification convention , such as specific identification of the goods , first - in - first - out ( FIFO ) , or average cost . Alternative systems may be used in some countries , such as last - in - first - out ( LIFO ) , gross profit method , retail method , or combinations of these .","entities":["damage","Costs","modification costs","supplies","additional material","supervision","use of equipment","allocation of costs","adjustments","decline in value of the goods","obsolescence","identification convention","first-in-first-out","last-in-first-out","gross profit method"],"facts":["damage -RELATED_TO-> Flash flooding","damage -RELATED_TO-> Downburst winds","damage -RELATED_TO-> DNA","animal studies -RELATED_TO-> damage","damage -RELATED_TO-> large hailstones"]}
