This is known as superfetation . Heteropaternal superfecundation in mammals ( edit ) Heteropaternal superfecundation is common in animals such as cats and dogs . Stray dogs can produce litters in which every puppy has a different sire . Though rare in humans , cases have been documented . In one study on humans , the frequency was 2.4 % among dizygotic twins whose parents had been involved in paternity suits . Cases involving superfecundation ( edit ) Koen and Teun Stuart , Dutch boys who were the result of in vitro fertilization ( IVF ) . In a mixup at the laboratory , equipment had been used twice , causing another man 's sperm to be mixed with the intended father 's . In 1995 , a young woman gave birth to diamniotic monochorionic twins , who were originally assumed to be monozygotic twins until a paternity suit led to a DNA test . This led to the discovery that the twins had different fathers .","entities":["equipment","Mammals","superfetation","in vitro fertilization","Sperm","humans","Heteropaternal superfecundation","cats","dogs","dizygotic twins","Koen Stuart","Teun Stuart","laboratory","another man","intended father"],"facts":["equipment -LOCATED_IN-> 19 steps below the roof","Firestone 500 Radial -RELATED_TO-> equipment","equipment -RELATED_TO-> bias-ply tires","Mammals -RELATED_TO-> Gallbladder","Mammals -RELATED_TO-> size"]}
