The Garamantes ' civilization eventually collapsed after they had depleted available water in the aquifers and could no longer sustain the effort to extend the tunnels further into the mountains . Berbers Zawiya at the entrance of Taghit , Algeria The Berber people occupied ( and still occupy ) much of the Sahara . The Garamantes Berbers built a prosperous empire in the heart of the desert . The Tuareg nomads continue to inhabit and move across wide Sahara surfaces to the present day . Islamic expansion Main articles : Trans - Saharan trade and Islamization of Sudan The Byzantine Empire ruled the northern shores of the Sahara from the 5th to the 7th centuries . After the Muslim conquest of Arabia , specifically the Arabian peninsula , the Muslim conquest of North Africa began in the mid-7th to early 8th centuries and Islamic influence expanded rapidly on the Sahara . By the end of 641 all of Egypt was in Muslim hands . Trade across the desert intensified , and a significant slave trade crossed the desert . It has been estimated that from the 10th to 19th centuries some 6,000 to 7,000 slaves were transported north each year .","entities":["North Africa","trans-Saharan trade","Muslims","slave trade","Byzantine Empire","Sahara","Algeria","Tuareg","Garamantes","Berbers","Muslim conquest of Arabia","Muslim conquest of North Africa","Byzantine Empire rule over the northern shores of the Sahara","Islamic expansion","Muslim control of Egypt"],"facts":["Libya -LOCATED_IN-> North Africa","Sahara Desert -LOCATED_IN-> North Africa","Afroasiatic -LOCATED_IN-> North Africa","Berber -LOCATED_IN-> North Africa","Deployment to North Africa -LOCATED_IN-> North Africa"]}
[Ed69354] {"title":"Sahara","text":"Sahara
Stone arrowheads , knives and scrapers from the era are commonly found . Burial items included pottery , jewelry , farming and hunting equipment , and assorted foods including dried meat and fruit . Burial in desert environments appears to enhance Egyptian preservation rites , and the dead were buried facing due west . By 3400 BCE , the Sahara was as dry as it is today , due to reduced precipitation and higher temperatures resulting from a shift in the Earth 's orbit . As a result of this aridification , it became a largely impenetrable barrier to humans , with the remaining settlements mainly being concentrated around the numerous oases that dot the landscape . Little trade or commerce is known to have passed through the interior in subsequent periods , the only major exception being the Nile Valley . The Nile , however , was impassable at several cataracts , making trade and contact by boat difficult . Phoenicians Further information : History of Western Sahara The people of Phoenicia , who flourished from 1200 -- 800 BCE , created a confederation of kingdoms across the entire Sahara to Egypt .","entities":["oases","Nile","Sahara","Nile Valley","Aridification of the Sahara","reduced precipitation","higher temperatures","shift in the Earth's orbit","remaining settlements","trade or commerce","Phoenicia","Phoenicians","confederation of kingdoms","Egypt"],"facts":["trade routes -RELATED_TO-> oases","Nile -RELATED_TO-> river's cataracts","Nile -LOCATED_IN-> Sudan","Nile -RELATED_TO-> North Africa","Nile -RELATED_TO-> Nilo-Saharan"]}
