The first music proper , a performance of the National Anthem was programmed by Christopher Strachey on the Mark II Manchester Electronic Computer at same venue , in 1951 . Later that year , short extracts of three pieces , the first being the National Anthem , were recorded there by a BBC outside broadcasting unit : the other pieces being `` Ba Ba Black Sheep , and `` In the Mood '' . Researchers at the University of Canterbury , Christchurch restored the acetate master disc in 2016 and the results may be heard on Soundcloud . Reception ( edit ) The philosopher and reformer Jeremy Bentham praised `` God Save the King '' in 1796 : `` the melody recommending itself by beauty to the most polished ears , and by its simplicity to the rudest ear . A song of this complexion , implanted by the habit of half a century in the mass of popular sentiment , can not be refused a place in the inventory of the national blessings . '' Ludwig van Beethoven wrote `` I have to show the English a little of what a blessing ' God Save the King ' is '' . Alex Marshall , the British author of Republic or Death !","entities":["Ludwig van Beethoven","Christopher Strachey","Mark II Manchester Electronic Computer","performance of the National Anthem","short extracts of three pieces","BBC outside broadcasting unit","Ba Ba Black Sheep","In the Mood","University of Canterbury","restoration of the acetate master disc","acetate master disc","Soundcloud","Jeremy Bentham","Republic or Death!","Alex Marshall"],"facts":["Marshall, Alex -CREATED-> Republic or Death!","Alex Marshall -CREATED-> Republic or Death!: Travels in Search of National Anthems","Ludwig van Beethoven -RELATED_TO-> Beethoven's Fifth Symphony","Ludwig van Beethoven -RELATED_TO-> Symphony No. 40","God Save the Queen -LOCATED_IN-> United Kingdom"]}
