The 1836 article and other sources make it clear that this verse was not used soon after 1745 , and certainly before the song became accepted as the British national anthem in the 1780s and 1790s . It was included as an integral part of the song in the Oxford Book of Eighteenth Century Verse of 1926 , although erroneously referencing the `` fourth verse '' to the Gentleman 's Magazine article of 1745 . On the opposing side , Jacobite beliefs were demonstrated in an alternative verse used during the same period : God bless the prince , I pray , God bless the prince , I pray , Charlie I mean ; That Scotland we may see Freed from vile Presbyt'ry , Both George and his Feckie , Ever so , Amen . In May 1800 , following an attempt to assassinate King George III at London 's Drury Lane theatre , playwright Richard Sheridan immediately composed an additional verse , which was sung from the stage the same night : From every latent foe From the assassins blow God save the King O'er him Thine arm extend For Britain 's sake defend Our father , king , and friend God save the King !","entities":["Scotland","George III","Britain","British national anthem","1836 article","Oxford Book of Eighteenth Century Verse","Gentleman's Magazine article","fourth verse","alternative verse","Jacobite beliefs","Presbyt'ry","attempt to assassinate King George III","Drury Lane theatre","Richard Sheridan","additional verse"],"facts":["Scotland -LOCATED_IN-> UK","Scotland -LOCATED_IN-> Europe","James -LOCATED_IN-> Scotland","1997 referendums in Scotland and Wales -LOCATED_IN-> Scotland","Scotland -LOCATED_IN-> United Kingdom"]}
