' The 2009 edition of Support for Spelling , by the English Department for Education , suggests an `` Extension activity '' for Year Five ( nine - year - olds ) : Children investigate the rule i before e except after c . Does this always apply ? What sound does ie make in these words ? In the Appendix , after a list of nine `` useful spelling guidelines '' , there is a note : The i before e except after c rule is not worth teaching . It applies only to words in which the ie or ei stands for a clear / ee / sound and unless this is known , words such as sufficient , veil and their look like exceptions . There are so few words where the ei spelling for the / ee / sound follows the letter c that it is easier to learn the specific words : receive , conceive , deceive ( + the related words receipt , conceit , deceit ) , perceive and ceiling . There were widespread media reports of this recommendation , which generated some controversy . The Oxford Dictionaries website of Oxford University Press states `` The rule only applies when the sound represented is ' ee ' , though .","entities":["Oxford University Press","Support for Spelling","English Department for Education","Oxford Dictionaries website","I before E except after C"],"facts":["Oxford University Press -LOCATED_IN-> Great Britain","Oxford University Press -LOCATED_IN-> United Kingdom","Oxford University Press -LOCATED_IN-> Melbourne","Oxford University Press -LOCATED_IN-> Oxford","Oxford University Press -LOCATED_IN-> New York"]}
