Alexander Hamilton proclaimed that no `` Citizen of the United States should refrain from turkey on Thanksgiving Day , '' and Benjamin Franklin had high regard for the wild turkey as an American icon , but turkey was uncommon as Thanksgiving fare until after 1800 . By 1857 , turkey had become part of the traditional dinner in New England . A Thanksgiving Day dinner served to the Civilian Conservation Corps in 1935 included : pickles , green olives , celery , roast turkey , oyster stew , cranberry sauce , giblet gravy , dressing , creamed asparagus tips , snowflake potatoes , baked carrots , hot rolls , fruit salad , mince meat pie , fruit cake , candies , grapes , apples , clams , fish , and many other food harvests . French drip coffee , cigars and cigarettes . The White House Cook Book , 1887 , by Mrs. F.L. Gillette , et al.","entities":["Alexander Hamilton","Benjamin Franklin","New England","Thanksgiving Day","wild turkey","American icon","traditional dinner in New England","Thanksgiving Day dinner served to the Civilian Conservation Corps","Civilian Conservation Corps","The White House Cook Book","Mrs. F.L. Gillette","turkey"],"facts":["Alexander Hamilton -RELATED_TO-> death of Alexander Hamilton","Alexander Hamilton -RELATED_TO-> Time","Alexander Hamilton -RELATED_TO-> Annapolis delegates","Alexander Hamilton -RELATED_TO-> Federalist No.","Alexander Hamilton -LOCATED_IN-> New York"]}
